name wordnet-data-verb
# wndb.5, "Data File Format": "Each data file begins with several lines containing a copyright notice, version number
# and license agreement. These lines all begin with two spaces and the line number. All other lines are in the following
# format. Integer fields are of fixed length, and are zero-filled.
#   synset_offset lex_filenum ss_type w_cnt word lex_id [word lex_id...] p_cnt [ptr...] [frames...] | gloss"
# "w_cnt: Two digit hexadecimal integer indicating the number of words in the synset"; "p_cnt: Three digit decimal
# integer indicating the number of pointers from this synset to other synsets"; each ptr is
# "pointer_symbol synset_offset pos source/target", "where synset_offset is the byte offset of the target synset in the
# data file corresponding to pos".
# The synset is the words it lists (word, "with spaces replaced by underscore characters"), and holds each of them.
# A synset's offset with the letter of its type is WordNet's key of it, which CILI names the ILI concept by
# (00001740-n): the offset is read as that concept, as index.sense and cntlist read sense keys, so a synset is typed
# as the concept it is and a pointer's target is the concept it names, in this file or another. The pointer_symbol is
# the relation, as the file writes it (@ Hypernym, ~ Hyponym, ...: wninput.5 "Pointers"). lex_filenum is lexnames'
# number of the lexicographer file. w_cnt and p_cnt count; lex_id is the source's identifier of a sense within a
# lexicographer file; pos is the key's letter: none is content. source/target (the word numbers a lexical pointer
# names) is left open here: it says which words, which these recipes do not yet read.
match data.verb

tier line by \n
comment space
names synset_offset lex_filenum ss_type w_cnt p_cnt f_cnt bar gloss
repeat word count w_cnt hex word lex_id
repeat pointers count p_cnt pointer_symbol synset_offset pos source_target
# "f_cnt + f_num w_num [+ f_num w_num...]": "f_cnt a two digit decimal integer indicating the number of generic frames
# listed, f_num is a two digit decimal integer frame number, and w_num is a two digit hexadecimal integer indicating the
# word in the synset that the frame applies to". "Each f_num w_num pair is preceded by a +." The frame numbers key
# wninput.5's generic frames (frames.vrb); f_num is left open until they are read.
repeat frames count f_cnt plus f_num w_num
tail gloss
tier field by space

thing word word
thing line of word
content word gloss ss_type pointer_symbol
omit w_cnt p_cnt bar lex_id pos f_cnt plus w_num
type lex_filenum lexfile
type synset_offset ili as \0-v
when pos is n
type pointers.synset_offset ili as \0-n
always
when pos is v
type pointers.synset_offset ili as \0-v
always
when pos is a
type pointers.synset_offset ili as \0-a
always
when pos is s
type pointers.synset_offset ili as \0-s
always
when pos is r
type pointers.synset_offset ili as \0-r
always
type pointers.synset_offset ili

holds line word
attest line synset_offset lex_filenum ss_type gloss
relate pointers pointer_symbol to synset_offset
